  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 AM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nAn expansive area of high pressure is currently situated over much \nof the Ohio Valley and central and southern Appalachians. The \nassociated strong subsidence is leading to mostly clear skies. \nFavorable radiational cooling will allow most places to fall to or a \nfew degrees below freezing tonight. \n\nAs upper level ridging continues to build towards the region this \nweek, a gradual warming trend is expected along with continued dry \nconditions. Temperatures will be nearing 5 to 10 degrees above \nnormal, though a slight uptick in cloud cover is possible, as models \nare starting to show signs of converging on a solution for a \ndeveloping area of low pressure to our south and east by the end of \nthe week and into the weekend. \n\nLatest iteration of the NBM has introduced some spotty low end PoP \nchances <= 30% associated with an increase in moisture advection \nover the weekend followed by a potential weak frontal passage in the \nSunday night/early Monday timeframe. Overall, rain threat looks \npretty benign and unlikely to produce impacts other than a slight \ncooldown with temperatures closer to seasonal norms next week.",
